steps.body:
A range-for over a map visits every entry. In these snippets `ages` is a `std::map<std::string, int>` from names to ages. Each element is a `std::pair<const Key, Value>`: `.first` is the key and `.second` is the value. The key is `const` because changing it would break the map's order.

```cpp harness
#include <bits/stdc++.h>

int main() {
    std::map<std::string, int> ages = {{"ana", 28}, {"Zoe", 35}, {"lee", 25}};
    {{code}}
    return 0;
}
```

```cpp
for (const auto& entry : ages) {
    std::cout << entry.first << ' ' << entry.second << '\n';
}
```

Structured bindings name both parts at once, which reads better:

```cpp harness
#include <bits/stdc++.h>

int main() {
    std::map<std::string, int> ages = {{"ana", 28}, {"Zoe", 35}, {"lee", 25}};
    {{code}}
    return 0;
}
```

```cpp
for (const auto& [name, age] : ages) {
    std::cout << name << ' ' << age << '\n';
}
```

A `std::map` keeps its entries in a sorted tree (a red-black tree in g++), so it always iterates in **sorted key order**, no matter the order you inserted in. Keys compare with `<`: numbers go ascending and strings by character code, so `"Zoe"` comes before `"ana"`. Each entry lives in its own heap node, so unlike a vector, adding or erasing other keys never moves it.

Use `const auto&` so each pair is read in place. `for (auto entry : ages)` copies every pair, strings included. Use `auto&` when you want to change values: `for (auto& [name, age] : ages) { ++age; }`.

