steps.body:
`find(key)` looks a key up without ever inserting. It returns an iterator to the entry, or `end()` when the key is missing. `it->second` is shorthand for `(*it).second`. Scope the iterator to the check with C++17's if with initializer. Here `ages` is a `std::map<std::string, int>` from names to ages:

```cpp harness
#include <bits/stdc++.h>

int main() {
    std::map<std::string, int> ages = {{"ana", 28}, {"Zoe", 35}, {"lee", 25}};
    {{code}}
    return 0;
}
```

```cpp
if (auto it = ages.find("ana"); it != ages.end()) {
    std::cout << it->first << ' ' << it->second << '\n';
}
```

This matters for `const` maps. `operator[]` may insert, so `ages["ana"]` on a `const` map or a `const&` parameter does not compile (g++ says it *discards qualifiers*). Read those with `find`, `at`, or `count`.

`insert` adds a pair only if the key is new. It never overwrites, and it returns a pair you can unpack: an iterator to the entry and a bool saying whether it inserted.

```cpp harness
#include <bits/stdc++.h>

int main() {
    std::map<std::string, int> ages = {{"ana", 28}, {"Zoe", 35}, {"lee", 25}};
    {{code}}
    std::cout << std::boolalpha << inserted << ' ' << it->second << '\n';
    return 0;
}
```

```cpp
auto [it, inserted] = ages.insert({"ana", 99});  // inserted is false
```

C++17 also has `try_emplace(k, v)`, which also never overwrites, and `insert_or_assign(k, v)`, which always writes. `erase(key)` removes a key and returns how many it removed (0 or 1). Finding, inserting, and erasing are all O(log n) in the tree.

example.code:
#include <iostream>
#include <map>
#include <string>

void printAge(const std::map<std::string, int>& ages, const std::string& name) {
    if (auto it = ages.find(name); it != ages.end()) {
        std::cout << it->first << ": " << it->second << '\n';
    } else {
        std::cout << name << ": unknown\n";
    }
}

int main() {
    std::map<std::string, int> ages = {{"sam", 31}, {"lee", 25}, {"ivy", 42}};
    printAge(ages, "sam");
    printAge(ages, "zoe");

    auto [it, inserted] = ages.insert({"sam", 40});
    std::cout << std::boolalpha << inserted << ' ' << it->second << '\n';

    ages.erase("lee");
    for (const auto& [name, age] : ages) {
        std::cout << name << ' ' << age << '\n';
    }
    return 0;
}

