Counting is where `[]` inserting a default shines. `++counts[key]` starts a new key at 0 and adds 1, so one line counts anything:

```cpp harness
#include <bits/stdc++.h>

int main() {
    const std::string text = "mississippi";
    {{code}}
    for (const auto& [letter, count] : counts) {
        std::cout << letter << ' ' << count << '\n';
    }
    return 0;
}
```

```cpp
std::map<char, int> counts;
for (char c : text) {
    ++counts[c];
}
```

`std::unordered_map` from `<unordered_map>` has the same interface (`[]`, `at`, `find`, `count`, `insert`, `erase`) but is a hash table: each key is hashed to a bucket, so operations are O(1) on average instead of O(log n) (O(n) in the rare worst case when many keys share a bucket). The price is order. Its iteration order is **unspecified** and can change between compilers or as it grows, so never print it directly. Copy the keys into a vector and sort them first:

```cpp harness
#include <bits/stdc++.h>

int main() {
    std::unordered_map<std::string, int> counts;
    for (const std::string& word : {"to", "be", "or", "not", "to", "be"}) {
        ++counts[word];
    }
    {{code}}
    for (const auto& word : keys) {
        std::cout << word << ' ' << counts.at(word) << '\n';
    }
    return 0;
}
```

```cpp
std::vector<std::string> keys;
for (const auto& entry : counts) {
    keys.push_back(entry.first);
}
std::sort(keys.begin(), keys.end());
```

Pick `std::map` when you need sorted order or the smallest or largest key. Pick `std::unordered_map` for the fastest lookups when order does not matter.

example.code:
#include <algorithm>
#include <iostream>
#include <map>
#include <string>
#include <unordered_map>
#include <vector>

int main() {
    const std::string text = "mississippi";
    std::map<char, int> letters;
    for (char c : text) {
        ++letters[c];
    }
    for (const auto& [letter, count] : letters) {
        std::cout << letter << ' ' << count << '\n';
    }

    const std::vector<std::string> words = {"to", "be", "or", "not", "to", "be"};
    std::unordered_map<std::string, int> wordCounts;
    for (const auto& word : words) {
        ++wordCounts[word];
    }
    std::vector<std::string> keys;
    for (const auto& entry : wordCounts) {
        keys.push_back(entry.first);
    }
    std::sort(keys.begin(), keys.end());
    for (const auto& word : keys) {
        std::cout << word << ' ' << wordCounts.at(word) << '\n';
    }
    return 0;
}

